  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 102 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nSunday morning, a closed upper low will be located over east central \nWyoming. A lead disturbance will bring likely POPs in showers and a \nfew thunderstorms to the western Sandhills into north central \nNebraska during the morning, followed by likely to categorical POPs \nfor showers and some thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening. By \nlate Sunday evening, the closed upper low will move into southwest \nsouth Dakota into north central Nebraska. NBM probability of greater \nthan 0.25 inch ending 12Z Monday is from 60 to over 90 percent for \nareas west of Imperial through Valentine. Highs Sunday will be \ncooler from the low 60s much of north central Nebraska to near 70 \nfar southwest. Persistent isentropic upglide will favor low cloud \nceilings on Sunday. On Monday, the upper low is forecast to move \nfrom nern NE/sern SD into Iowa. Low chance POPs for rain showers \nwill linger mainly across north central NE. Upper ridging will build \ninto the region Tuesday into Tuesday night. Lows chances for showers \nand a few afternoon thunderstorms as well, mainly across the western \nSandhills. \n\nWednesday into Wednesday night, a slight chance to low chance POPs \nfor showers and a few thunderstorms as subtropical moisture \nfrom the Four Corners region lifts northeast and tops the upper \nridge over Nebraska. Precipitation looks light and spotty. \nMainly dry conditions Thursday.\n\nHighs Monday and Tuesday will remain cool, mainly in the 60s, \nwarming to 65 to 75 Wednesday and the 70s Thursday.",
